  "abstract": "Quadratic interpolation determinants depend on local divisibility of weighted Hasse columns in an original triangular polynomial space. We complete the exact universal index formula for every positive integer slope and prove stronger lower bounds for a finite derivative budget.\n\nLet $q=p^2$, $\\alpha(m)=\\sum_{\\ell\\ge1}\\lfloor m/q^\\ell\\rfloor$, and let the rows satisfy $r+Ks<R$, with column price $\\lfloor j/p\\rfloor$. For all derivative orders, the exact row credit is $\\lfloor r/p\\rfloor+\\alpha(s)$ when $K\\le p$, the previously proved $s+\\alpha(\\lfloor r/p\\rfloor)$ when $p<K\\le p^3$, and $\\lfloor r/p^3\\rfloor+s+\\alpha(s)$ when $K>p^3$. A finite digit-algebra basis proves saturation in the complementary chamber, using the classical digit principle and the preceding Frobenius ghost construction.\n\nFor $K\\ge p^3$, local $p$-ordering products give a finite-jet bound by counting surviving derivative factors. A further construction integrates derivatives of powers of $x^q-x$ and controls every retained jet. When $R\\le q^2J$, its additional index is an explicit shifted floor sum. Both constructions preserve the original support and column prices.\n\nAt the two unchanged contracts of the accepted $D\\le331$ theorem, the complete arithmetic improvements, including the previously established other-prime gains, exceed $0.0092944$ and $0.0092813$. Exact coefficient modules, rational polynomial identities and quadratic residue-ring checks support the proofs. The digit-basis saturation step has a separate independent mathematical audit. No rank, metric, composite or endpoint certificate is recomputed; no new discriminant bound, census or classification is claimed. The finite-jet statements are lower bounds, not exact index formulas.",
